<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">March First Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Samil Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March First Movement was a nationwide Korean protest against Japanese colonial rule that began on 1 March 1919 with the reading of a Declaration of Independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. Hundreds of thousands joined peaceful demonstrations, which the Japanese authorities suppressed with thousands of deaths. It led to the founding of the Provisional Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-first-movement.jpg" alt="March First Movement" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bureau d'information coréen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%E2%80%99Ind%C3%A9pendance_de_la_Cor%C3%A9e_et_la_Paix-02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> formally annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Japanese rule was at first especially tight: Japan took control of the Korean economy and began a process of forced cultural assimilation, land was confiscated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and given to Japanese people, and freedom of speech and the press were tightly controlled. The devastation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> led intellectuals in Korea and elsewhere to discuss anti-colonialism and liberal reform. Korean students in Japan, exposed to liberal democracy through Japan’s Taishō Democracy movement and Wilson’s Fourteen Points, issued a declaration of independence on 8 February. In late 1918 leaders of the Korean religion Cheondoism, including Son Byong-hi, concluded that nonviolent resistance would best win international opinion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Around noon on 1 March 1919, 29 of the 33 signers of the Declaration of Independence gathered in the T’aehwagwan restaurant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and read it aloud. The owner reported the gathering to the Japanese Government-General, and about 80 military police arrested the signers. Similar protests were held the same day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, Chinnamp’o, Anju and Wonsan, and by 19 March all thirteen provinces had hosted protests. All but seven of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>’s 218 administrative districts did so. Estimates run to about 1,500 to 1,800 protests. They were decentralised and shaped by local religion: Christians led in some regions and Cheondoists in others. The Government-General brought in more police, military police and army divisions, armed with lethal weapons.</p><p>Suppression was often violent. A Korean estimate of 1920 claimed 7,509 deaths and 46,948 arrests, while Japanese authorities reported much lower numbers. Yu Gwan-sun, a sixteen-year-old student, became a symbol of the movement. On 1 April the military police fired on 3,000 protesters at Cheonan and killed 19, among them her parents. Foreign missionaries such as Frank Schofield documented the events for the press abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The movement did not bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>’s liberation, but it invigorated the independence movement. Korean exiles who gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> founded the Korean Provisional Government in April 1919, now considered a predecessor of the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. The colonial government made limited concessions known as cultural rule, which allowed Korean newspapers such as The Dong-A Ilbo and The Chosun Ilbo to be founded. Chinese writers praised the protests, though historians debate how far the movement influenced other protests. In South Korea 1 March is a national holiday, and the constitution’s first sentence refers to the movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “March First Movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protesters sang it during the <b>March First Movement</b> of 1919, and mountain guerrillas fighting the occupiers sang it too.</blockquote><a href="/songs/arirang">Arirang</a></li><li><blockquote>In Korea it was used as a protest song in the <b>March First Movement</b> against Japanese rule in 1919.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li><li><blockquote>In the wake of the <b>March First Movement</b>, independence activists met in Shanghai's French Concession and in April 1919 proclaimed the Republic of Korea with a provisional constitution and assembly.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-korea">Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea</a></li></ul></section>
